# Line endings are LF in the repository, and LF in the working tree.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Without it, a checkout on Windows — or an edit from a Windows editor against
# a shared volume — rewrites files as CRLF. Git then reports every line of
# every touched file as changed. It happened here: fourteen files, 4,982
# insertions and 4,982 deletions, and not one character of real difference.
# A diff that size does not get read, which is precisely how a genuine change
# rides into a commit unnoticed.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate rather than `eol=native`. The scripts in this repo run
# in Linux containers, and a CRLF shebang line fails with a message that names
# the interpreter rather than the line ending, which is a bad afternoon.

# Detect text automatically, normalise to LF in the index, check out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and SQL are executed by tools that will not tolerate CR.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf

# Explicit rather than inferred, so `text=auto`'s heuristic is never consulted.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.prisma text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are generated. Collapsing them in review keeps a dependency bump
# from burying the change that prompted it.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
